what comes to your mind when you think  about persuasion some sort of commercial  message urging you to purchase a certain  product or a political candidate  attempting to persuade you to put their  name on the ballot but let us tell you  persuasion is not only limited to these  two things  it's an integral part of our daily lives  many times we believe that we are  impervious to persuasion and we have an  innate capacity to see through a sales  pitch perceive the truth of the  situation and draw our own judgments but  that is not the mere reality according  to psychology certain factors impact our  decision making tremendously  so today in this video we will delve  over the five principles of persuasion  psychology in detail but before we begin  make sure to subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell icon to never  miss an update from simply learn  we all live in a fast-paced world due to  which we rely on shortcuts or rules of  thumb more than ever to guide our  decision making  these shortcuts are the common  persuasion psychology traits that  influence one's decision making if you  are wondering what persuasion means let  me tell you that it is the process by  which a person's attitude or behavior  are influenced by communications from  another person  so moving ahead let's discover the  principles of persuasion one by one  the first principle we have is  reciprocity to some extent we humans  value equality and balance  this means we don't enjoy feeling like  we owe other people  for instance if someone gives you a  birthday present you will certainly want  to send them one in return  you'll do this when their birthday rolls  in next to satisfy your feeling of  social obligation  so now you must be thinking about how  this can be applied in a business  environment well think about the last  time that you went to a restaurant  you must have received a small gift from  the waiter about the same time they  brought your bill a small mint perhaps  does that small gift affect the amount  of tip you gave them  you'll probably say no but research  shows that providing a single mint  boosts the tip by three percent  furthermore if the waiter offers another  mint saying that here's one more mint  for you good people then the tip climbs  about 23 percent  this means the amount of tip is  independent of the value of gift and  rather it is dependent on the way it is  presented the key to using this  principle of reciprocation is to be the  first to give and ensure that what you  give is personalized and unexpected  the next principle is scarcity  the less of something there is the more  people tend to want it for example  consider the scenario of iphone 13 pro  devices  due to the ongoing worldwide  semiconductor shortage apple announced  the cut in production of 10 million  iphone 13 pro devices  after this announcement the pre-booking  and sales of this mobile phone went  through the roof increasing time by  about 15 to 20 days  so with this example the science behind  the principle of scarcity is pretty  clear  you will have to create a sense of  scarcity around the product you want to  sell  it is not enough just to highlight the  benefits of your product you will have  to tell people what is so unique about  it and what they might stand to lose if  they fail to consider your proposal  immediately  the third principle on our list is  authority  individuals who are authoritative  knowledgeable and reputed in their  respective fields are more influential  and persuasive than those who are not  part of the justification for this is  that authority and credibility are  fundamental components of trust and we  are more prone to follow someone we  trust  in many realms of life we witness the  principle of authority in action  dentists in white coats are used to  promote toothpaste the camera  capabilities of mobile phones are  marketed with the help of well-known  movie directors  even in the corporate environment  strings of signatures are attached in  the email to improve an individual's  authority  you can achieve this in your business by  recommending others so that they feel a  social obligation to recommend you in  return  the fourth principle of persuasion  psychology is consensus  humans are social by nature and they  typically believe that adhering to the  conventions of a social group is crucial  this belongingness to social proof  always impact human behavior and  decision making  hotel towels are a great illustration of  this persuasion principle  during your stay at the hotel you must  have come across small cards in your  rooms asking you to reuse the towels for  the sake of environmental protection  it turns out this is a very effective  method resulting in nearly 34 compliance  but could there be a more effective way  well the research shows that on adding  the principle of consensus and bending  the words on the message card as 8 out  of 10 guests who stays in this room  choose to reuse their towel can lead to  a 52 increase in the reuse of towels  however this principle of consensus is  challenging to apply from a personal  perspective in the workplace  you'll have to achieve this by  maintaining your brand's reputation and  ethics the last principle we have on our  list is liking  given human nature people are much more  likely to like people that are similar  to them who pay compliments and who  cooperate with them than those who don't  according to cialdini's research a group  of mba students who were asked to get  straight down to business were able to  reach an agreement at a rate of 55  whereas students who were encouraged to  discuss their personal interests before  negotiation were able to reach an  agreement 90 of the time this is simply  due to the sharing of personal interests  and the creation of a sense of  likeliness to consider this in the form  of a business context take the example  of apple stores it's no surprise that  this high-end high-tech apple store is  manned by representatives dressed up in  jeans and t-shirts rather than typical  suits and ties  apple uses the liking principle to its  advantage  this strategy of apple implies i am a  human being just like you i'm casual and  approachable  this strategy creates a sense of  belonging in customers to purchase  apple's products these principles that  we discussed provide for small ethically  sound and often costless changes that  can lead to substantial alterations in  your capacity to convince others to buy  your products  hence conduct a thorough analysis of  these psychological principles and  implement them in your work life that's  all we have for today we wish you all  the very best for your future management  endeavors thank you for watching this  video and stay tuned for more from  simply learn  hi there if you like this video  subscribe to the simply learn youtube  channel and click here to watch similar  videos turn it up and get certified  click here
 

